CAMERA HLS SESSION LIFECYCLE
===========================

The API process that starts an HLS session owns its FFmpeg child and private
output files. That process checks for stale sessions every 15 seconds. A session
with no heartbeat, playlist fetch or valid segment fetch for more than 30 seconds
is eligible for cleanup, even if its viewer count is still positive. Browser
crashes and lost network connections cannot reliably send a final viewer-stop
request. A positive viewer count is therefore not evidence of current activity.

Normal heartbeat and media polling keep the session alive. Explicitly stopping
the final viewer releases it immediately. The six-hour maximum session age is
still enforced independently of recent activity. The idle check is periodic,
so cleanup is not promised at exactly the 30-second boundary.

Known boundary: HLS session metadata is currently local to one API process.
A different API worker cannot look up, heartbeat or stop that session even when
it shares the output directory. This is a reproduced limitation, not a qualified
multi-worker playback implementation. It is separate from go2rtc playback paths.
Do not treat successful page rendering, an existing playlist file, or tests of
one worker as acceptance of HLS playback across workers or hosts. Cross-worker
ownership/routing and owner-loss recovery remain release qualification work.

The idle cleanup correction does not resolve that ownership limitation. Nor does
it make a one-worker deployment a qualified availability workaround: worker
recycling has a separately observed request delay and terminates owned streams.
